# Two characters walk past each other and back; the sync carries both.
# Same-tick divergence includes the latency and the playback delay by design: at walking speed
# 3.6 m/s times one way (50 ms) plus the longest delay (15 ticks, 250 ms) is 1.1 m. The
# lag-aligned divergence takes that out: what is left is the sync's own error. 1.1 m is
# derived; lag_divergence.p95 <= 0.1m is measured (0.056 m), with a margin.
scenario two_walk
scene two_chars_item
seed 7
guests 1
settle 3s

net g1 rtt 100ms jitter 10ms loss 2%

p0 walk 2 1
p0 wait 500ms
p0 walk -2 0 as p0_home
p1 walk -2 -1 speed 2.5
p1 walk 2 0 as p1_home
at 6s p1 walk box as p1_box

expect result p0_home done
expect result p1_home done
expect result p1_box done
expect agree_at_rest 5cm 3deg
expect no_stuck
expect host_ticks.max <= 30t
expect divergence.p95 <= 1.1m
expect lag_divergence.p95 <= 0.1m
# behind_ticks: a copy plays at most delay_max (15 ticks, before the clock knows the path)
# behind its owner's newest, plus the one-way delay and the jitter in ticks, plus 2 for the
# tick the packet arrives in and the tick the owner's newest is read after its step:
# rtt 100 ms (3) and 10 ms jitter (1): 15 + 3 + 1 + 2 = 21. Derived.
expect behind_ticks.max <= 21t
